# Untitled

## Preparation of Highly Moisture-Resistant Black-Colored Metal Organic Frameworks
Seung Jae Yang and Chong Rae Park*
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are a leading class of microporous materials that are useful in a wide range of applications, including gas storage and separation, catalysis, sensors, and drug delivery.[1–5] The porosity of MOFs can be tuned and the functional groups on their surfaces can be tailored to favor specific chemical interactions. MOFs are easily prepared with relatively high synthetic yields, making them promising candidates for commercialization.[3,6,7] In practice, MOFs have the disadvantage that, in the presence of moisture, they are unstable and lose their high specific surface area (SSA), even under ambient atmospheric conditions.[8–12] Enhancing the moisture-resistance of MOFs is therefore crucial for practical applications of MOFs.
Among the MOFs reported to date, zinc-based MOFs, particularly isoreticular MOF-1 (IRMOF-1: conventionally denoted MOF-5), are the most moisture-sensitive because their relatively weak metal–oxygen coordination bonds are vulnerable to hydrolysis, which destroy the MOF open framework structure.[12] In an attempt to mitigate this problem, Nguyen et al. introduced hydrophobic alkyl chains onto an amine-containing IRMOF-1 (denoted IRMOF-3) in a post-synthetic step.[13] Yang et al. reported that the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of methyl \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\right)\)- and dimethyl \(\left(\mathrm{DiCH}_{3}\right)\)-modified IRMOF-1 were preserved after several days of exposure to ambient moisture.[14] Recently, Decoste et al. reported the plasma-enhanced chemical vapor deposition (PECVD) of perfluorohexane as a moisture-resistant surface modification of HKUST-1 \(\left(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\right)\), where BTC denotes 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid;[15] however, despite yielding excellent moisture-resistance, these approaches require quite complicated synthetic steps (chemical modifications) and are applicable only to specific MOFs (PECVD technique).
Here we report, for the first time, that carbon-coating IRMOF-1 yields a remarkable enhancement in moisture resistance. In previous studies, "carbonaceous grease", which is more hydro-stable than MOFs, has been incorporated into such frameworks to help stabilize them against hydrolysis.[9,11] Recently, a variety of MOFs have been widely exploited as templates or precursors for nanoporous carbonaceous materials.[16,18] In the intermediate state of the transformation, a
simple heat treatment at a specific temperature covered the frameworks in an amorphous carbon coating, which shielded the direct exposure of MOFs to moisture, thereby preventing hydrolysis (see Figure 1). Higher temperatures produced more moisture-resistant MOFs because thicker carbon coatings could be introduced; however, overheating produced a ZnO@carbon material that did not display microporosity or the chemical characteristics of MOFs.
Raman spectroscopy was used to characterize the physicochemical states of IRMOF-1 and the thermally modified MOF surfaces because the XRD patterns of all products indicated similar surface crystal structures (Figure S1). The samples were designated using a code comprised of the heating temperature (in \(^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)) followed by the atmospheric conditions; for example, \(480\,\mathrm{~N~}\) denotes a sample heated at \(480\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under a nitrogen atmosphere. The strong bands observed at 635, 863, 1137, 1433, and \(1613\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) were consistent with the Raman bands reported by Yaghi and Bordiga et al.,[19,20] which were associated with the vibrational modes of the organic benzene rings and the carboxylate groups (Figure 2a). The 510N sample displayed two distinct bands at 1320 and \(1590\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), assigned, respectively, to the typical D and G bands of amorphous carbon (the transparent surface appeared black after coating).[18,21] This result indicated that the surface organic ligands of the IRMOF-1 had reorganized to form an amorphous carbon coating. These bands become more prominent with increasing temperature. The 550N sample displayed bands associated with both ZnO nanocrystals (below \(600\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\)) and amorphous carbon (Figure S2).[22,23] Combined with the XRD results, the inherent crystal structure of IRMOF-1 was retained after thermal modification, although the surfaces of the thermally modified MOFs were transformed into an amorphous carbon layer.
The textural characteristics of the products were quantified by measuring the cryogenic nitrogen sorption curves, as shown in Figures S3 and 2c. The IRMOF-1 sample displayed a typical microporous type-I isotherm with a steep increase at low pressure. After thermal modification, the isotherm decreased at low pressure and increased at intermediate and high pressures, indicating a reduction in the microporosity and the concurrent development of mesoporosity. The decrease in microporosity upon thermal modification reduced the BET SSAs
from 3450 (IRMOF-1) to \(1740\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) (530N); this change can be attributed to the development of a mesoporous amorphous carbon layer (550N) on the IRMOF-1 surface (Figure S4). It is noteworthy that the pore size distributions (PSDs) of all products were similar, regardless of the degree of thermal modification (Figure 2d). These results, together with the XRD findings, demonstrated that the overall crystal structure and inherent pore characteristics of the IRMOF-1 samples were preserved during thermal treatment until the sample had completely transformed into ZnO@amorphous carbon (550N).
The desolvated MOFs were exposed to ambient air (34% relative humidity) and subsequently evaluated by measuring their powder XRD (PXRD) patterns to gain insight into the crystal structure in the presence of moisture (Figure 3a).[9,13,15] Exposure of the desolvated IRMOF-1 to air for 3 days resulted in a change in the relative peak intensities and the appearance of new peaks, indicating commencement of hydrolysis (Figure S5).[10,11] Further exposure to air resulted in the near-complete disappearance of the initial patterns, suggesting an almost complete phase transformation. In contrast, the patterns of all thermally modified MOFs remained essentially unchanged, even after 14 days of exposure, with no new peaks and no reduction in the peak intensity (Figures S6–8). Thus, the amorphous carbon coating introduced via thermal modification protected the moisture-sensitive IRMOF-1 surface.
The SSA was measured as a function of the exposure time to quantify the degree of moisture resistance resulting from the thermal modification (Figure 3b, Table 1). Prior to performing the adsorption test, all samples were exposed to ambient air and then activated at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(20\,\mathrm{h}\) to remove any surface-adsorbed impurities. The nitrogen adsorption isotherms of the IRMOF-1 exposed to ambient air showed a substantial decrease in the BET SSA from 3450 to \(960\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) (28% SSA retained); by
In contrast, the thermally-modified MOFs displayed significantly better SSA retention after 14 days of exposure to ambient air (86%, 91%, and 99%, for the 480N, 510N, and 530N samples, respectively), demonstrating an enhanced stability in the presence of moisture (Figure S9–12).
The effects of a carbon layer coating on the stability of the MOFs in terms of resistance to hydrolysis were further tested
by immersing the IRMOF-1 and 510N samples in water, as shown in Figure 4a. The well-faceted cubic crystals of IRMOF-1 rapidly hydrolyzed to form powders within 10 s and, consequently, the transparent water rapidly became turbid, indicative of moisture-sensitive MOFs. In contrast, the 510N sample was not hydrolyzed; the water above the 510N sample remained clear and colorless for the duration of the soak time (2 h). The XRD patterns of the 510N sample after \(2\,\mathrm{h}\) of soaking in water indicated that the crystal structure remained intact. After soaking the IRMOF-1 sample in water, the crystal structure had changed completely. These results suggest that the amorphous carbon surface that formed during heat treatment hindered the hydrolysis of the MOFs, even against submersion in water.
In summary, we have shown that thermal modification of IRMOF-1 remarkably enhanced the stability of the framework against hydrolysis. Specifically, heat treatment of the IRMOF-1 samples led to the formation of an amorphous carbon coating on their surfaces, thus shielding the framework from decomposition under humid conditions. The thermally modified MOFs retained their crystal structure and pore characteristics, even after 14 days of exposure to ambient air. This scalable and environmentally friendly modification method opens new opportunities for the commercial preparation of water-resistant MOFs.
## Experimental Section
Reagents and chemicals: Zinc nitrate tetrahydrate (Merck), terephthalic acid (Aldrich), \(N,N^{\prime}\)-dimethylformamide (DMF; Daejung, Korea), \(N,N^{\prime}\)-diethylformamide (DEF; Merck), and anhydrous chloroform (Aldrich) were used without purification.
Preparation of IRMOF-1: Zinc nitrate tetrahydrate \((0.78\ \mathrm{g},\ 3\ \mathrm{mmol})\) and terephthalic acid \((0.17\ \mathrm{g},\ 1\ \mathrm{mmol})\) were dissolved in DEF \((30\ \mathrm{mL})\) in a vial. The reaction mixture was heated in a furnace at \(105\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\ \mathrm{h}\) to yield large cubic crystals of IRMOF-1. The reaction vessel was removed from the furnace and allowed to cool to room temperature. The cubic crystals were repeatedly washed with DMF and anhydrous chloroform, soaked in anhydrous chloroform for \(12\ \mathrm{h}\), filtered, and vacuum-dried at pressures less than \(10^{-3}\) Torr at \(150\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\ \mathrm{h}\).
Preparation of thermally-modified MOFs: The prepared MOFs were transferred to a tube furnace and were heat-treated at the target temperature under nitrogen at a heating rate of \(10\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}\) to pyrolyze the MOF surfaces. After reaching the target temperature, the materials were immediately cooled to room temperature.
Characterizations: Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) was conducted using a D8 Advance (Bruker) diffractometer. Diffractograms were recorded in reflection mode using Ni-filtered CuKα radiation \((\lambda=0.154184\ \mathrm{nm})\). Raman spectroscopy was conducted using a RAMANplus confocal laser Raman microscope (Nanophoton) with 532 and \(785\ \mathrm{nm}\) laser wavelengths to analyze the surface chemical structure of the products. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was conducted under a nitrogen flow, using an SDT Q600 instrument (TA Instruments). Volumetric nitrogen adsorption isotherms (at \(77\ \mathrm{K}\)) up to 1 bar were measured using a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 static volumetric gas adsorption instrument. All sorption isotherms were obtained using ultrahigh purity gases \((99.999\%)\). Prior to the sorption analysis, samples \((0.1-0.2\ \mathrm{g})\) in the analysis chamber were subject to a vacuum of \(10^{-5}\) Torr at \(150\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\ \mathrm{h}\). The nitrogen adsorption data were used to calculate the apparent surface areas based on the BET equation. The linearized BET model was fit to the microporous MOF data within the relative pressure range \(0.001<\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{P}_{0}<0.05\), as described.[24] Horvath–Kawazoe pore size distributions were determined using the cylindrical pore model.
